# Claim template. Copy to docs/claims/<name>.claim, replace every <placeholder>,
# and delete these comment lines: a claim file holds only "key = value" lines
# (scripts/validate-kv.sh rejects comments and blank lines), and
# scripts/check-claims.sh validates every *.claim file.
#
# A claim is a public statement of what a named body of evidence shows. It is
# bound to a profile, a content identity, a startup tier, an evidence tier and the
# exact test inventory it was made against.
#
# Same-core digests are repeatability (R), not accuracy: a digest produced twice by
# this project's own core shows the machine is deterministic, and says nothing about
# whether it matches hardware. Only tiers T1 to T7 evidence supports an accuracy
# claim, and none exists yet.
#
# claim             one sentence stating exactly what the evidence shows.
# profile           the hardware profile the claim is about. "none" while owner
#                   decision O1 (baseline model) is open; check-claims.sh rejects any
#                   other value until the allowed list is extended.
# content_sha256    SHA-256 (64 lowercase hex digits) of the content bytes run,
#                   from the content_sha256 line of the run's run.manifest.
# startup_tier      the startup route, from the run manifest (A: direct load; none for a software-model vector).
# evidence_tier     T1 to T7, or R for repeatability.
# inventory_sha256  SHA-256 of tests/inventory/fast.txt, over its exact bytes and
#                   order. Any change to the inventory makes the claim stale.
#                   Print the current value with:
#                     sh scripts/check-claims.sh --inventory-sha256
#                   Update every claim to it only after re-running the evidence.
# evidence          the test names or bundle files that back the claim.
#
# A future CPU vector claim (Phase 2 onward) sets content_sha256 to the SHA-256 of
# the vector inventory file tests/inventory/vectors/<profile>.txt, sets
# startup_tier = none (a software-model vector injects CPU state and is not tier A)
# and evidence_tier T1, and claims a software model on a flat test bus only: no
# timing, no interrupt and no memory-map claim. A claim that names a profile
# (lynx1 or lynx2) waits for owner decision O1.
claim = <one sentence>
profile = <none until O1 is decided>
content_sha256 = <64 hex>
startup_tier = <A>
evidence_tier = <T1..T7 or R>
inventory_sha256 = <sha256 of tests/inventory/fast.txt>
evidence = <bundle or test names>
